// ===================== 10.3 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('10.3  Nominalise and un-nominalise at will'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **The second half of the question is the one that matters: _who is the actor_, and _nobody_ is an allowed answer.** **In most classes five or six of the ten are _nobody_** — **and about half of those five are right**, which is the finding and which no class expects. **Write the ten actors and leave the _nobodies_ unmarked until 3R.**'));
out.push(A('**b** Should stay: **4** and **5**. ⚠ **4 is a consequence and has no actor at all** — _the withdrawal of the claim does not affect the other findings_ — **and 5 is a rule, where the actor is everybody.** **1 and 3 are events with dates and actors who matter; 2 is already active.** **Make the room say what supplying an actor would do to 5**: it turns a rule into a decision somebody took about somebody.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **it was a rule for writers who had only one direction**, and at C1 the move goes both ways and is chosen on the reader.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **The un-nominalised version was better English and he did not send it** — **because the first version is about a rule and the second is about a family.**   **2** **In a rule there is no actor because the rule applies to everybody**, and supplying one turns the rule into a decision somebody took about somebody.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Say at the start of this cycle that B2 was not wrong, and say why, or the class will conclude that it was.** **At B2 the fault runs one way: a writer nominalises to sound official and the advice to un-nominalise is correct for them.** **A level teaches a one-directional rule when its students have one fault** — **and the C1 move is the same rule with the second direction added, not a reversal of it.** ⚠ **Two or three students will still hear it as a reversal.** **The repair is Mr Tayara\'s letter: the un-nominalised version is better English and was not sent**, and the question that decided it was not about grammar.'));
out.push(NOTE('**The deciding question is three seconds long and must be written on the board in these words.** ⚠ _Who is helped by the actor being there?_ **Not _is this good English_, and not _is this clear_** — **both of those have the same answer every time and neither decides anything.** ⚠ **Then the rule nobody writes down, which is Mr Zahreddine\'s and should be given with his name on it: findings active, boundaries nominal.** _Fifty-five positions read 12.0 to 12.2_ **is an event with an actor who matters.** _The comparison establishes a difference and not a date_ **is a consequence with no actor at all.** **He has written this way for twenty years without a name for it, and naming it is most of what this cycle does for the room.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(A('**1** **A rule:** _non-attendance on three consecutive days triggers a letter._ **Keep it nominal.**   **2** **A protocol with three readers:** _administration is to be discontinued._ **Keep it: each of the three is the actor.**   **3** **A consequence:** _the withdrawal of the claim does not affect the other findings._ **Keep it.**   **4** **An event:** _the committee condensed the four paragraphs on 3 May._ **Active, with the date.**   **5** ⚠ **An instruction to one reader:** _switch it off and on again._ **Always active.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
