out.push(SAMPLE(['The phrase Mrs Oueidat could not carry was to all intents and purposes. An expert had written that the file was, to all intents and purposes, complete, and there is no form of words in Arabic that carries exactly that: it means complete enough for the present purpose and not complete, and it means both at once. Mrs Tarabishi asked what she did, and she rendered it as complete for the purposes of this report, and the court heard a flat statement that the file was complete — which the expert had not said and would not have said. Mrs Khoury said this is the thing a writer can do and a translator cannot: you find it before anybody needs it, because a translator who meets it has to decide in about four seconds between losing the hedge and adding a sentence that is not yours, and nine times in ten they lose the hedge. Dr Qabbani asked whether it is always idiom, and three groups, and idiom is the easiest: idiom goes with a rewrite, an institutional word goes with a gloss, and a hedge sometimes cannot go at all. Mr Tayara asked whether form tutor really has no equivalent, and none, and a translator will choose a word from their own system and the reader will then picture a person with different powers — and five words from the writer prevents it. Mr Shihabi said a wire story is translated by somebody he will never meet, and four or five times within a day. Mrs Ajouz asked about satisfactory, and satisfactory is the worst word in this book and it is not an idiom: it has an exact equivalent in every language and means something different in each institution, so the translation is perfect and the sense is gone. Mrs Rizk asked how to find them if nothing looks difficult, and read the sentence and ask whether you would have to explain it to somebody in another office — not another language, another office. Mr Attar said a manual is translated by a machine now, and a machine has no way of asking, which makes it the strictest reader in the book. Mr Zahreddine said his opinions are translated and he has never been asked a question. And Mrs Oueidat said the thing she will take away is that she decided on his behalf: five seconds, in a hearing, with nobody to ask, and the expert sat through a sentence of his own that said something he had been careful not to say.']));
