out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Shihabi has eighty words and forty minutes, and the forty minutes are not spent writing: about five minutes writing and thirty-five on the telephone, because the eighty words are made of counts and denominators and dates and he does not have them when the story starts. Mrs Tarabishi asked what is on the page at minute five, and the shape, with gaps in it — two studies put repayment at so many per cent and differ about something — and then he knows which two telephone calls to make, and the calls are short because he knows exactly what he is asking for. Mrs Khoury said that is the only way eighty words gets written and it is the opposite of how everybody is taught: the draft is a list of holes, and a writer who drafts the sentences first spends the forty minutes making the prose good and files eighty words with no figures in them, and a desk cannot put figures into a story. Dr Qabbani asked what happens when they arrive with nothing wrong with them, and she reads them twice and sends them, which happens about once a week: the second read is for sense and not for faults, and the difference between the two reads is about four minutes of hers and a whole round of the writer\'s. Mr Tayara asked whether eighty is a real number, and it is the space — four paragraphs of twenty words, and the desk cuts from the bottom, so the fourth paragraph is written knowing it will go and the first carries both positions. Mrs Ajouz said nothing she writes has a space limit, and Mrs Khoury said everything she writes has a reader limit, which is the same thing arriving later. Mrs Rizk said four paragraphs for a proposal is absurd, and write the eighty words first and then the proposal: if the eighty cannot be written, the proposal has nothing in it, which is true about a third of the time. Mr Attar asked what goes in the first twenty words, and the thing that is new with its number and the thing it is in dispute with, and not the background. Mr Zahreddine said an opinion cannot be written in eighty words, and Mrs Oueidat said the covering letter can and it is the only part the insurer reads. And Mr Shihabi said the thing he will take away is the thirty-five minutes: for two years he thought he was a slow writer, and he was a fast writer with nothing to write about.']));
